my iPad for the most part has replaced  my Sketchbook and so in this video I'm  going to show you three different ways  that I use my iPad for work  [Music]  hey guys welcome back to the channel my  name is dammy and I am an architect in  beautiful Vancouver BC on this channel  we talk about architecture and design  and also some tools and strategies that  can help us have more meaningful and  fulfilling careers in architecture  paper is still a very big part of my  workflow especially when I'm working  with my colleagues or when we're doing  design reviews and I do think there's  certain things that paper and a fat  marker just can't beat but after seeing  some other Architects like Henry GAO or  Eric Reinhold transformed their workflow  with the iPad I decided to get one for  myself and over the last year or so it's  become a really essential part of my  workflow I use a combination of  procreate notability click up and PDF  for work and I'm going to show you how I  use that here  one of the ways I maximize the use of my  iPad as an architect is with these  graphic templates I created these  templates and brush presets based on the  things I use every single day and I've  got things from people and tree brushes  to scale paper templates basically every  time I start a new project I start with  this and it saves me tons and tons of  time all of these templates that I'm  using in this video are going to be  available at the link in the description  so the first way I use the iPad is for  design iterations as a design tool in  the earlier phases of the design so when  you're in the schematic phases of the  design you don't really want to spend  too much time fully developing one  option you want to try doing multiple  iterations and spend that time to  develop multiple rough options and  that's because you can have a much  better discussion this way like you can  talk about the pros and cons of each  option and you can collaboratively pick  an option to develop more in detail  which is why in this phase Architects  tend to draw things out on paper because  you can think faster and draw faster  than doing it on the computer but the  problem with this of course is that hand  sketched floor plans are not nearly as  accurate as floor plans made on cat or  Revit and it gets messy very fast I  actually used to spend a lot of time  just drawing and redrawing things  because even though the ideas were  worked out oh it just didn't look clean  enough to be presentable and I think we  can all agree the presentability of the  thing has a lot to do with how your work  is perceived then there's the scanning  and printing again and there's just a  lot of wasted time and paper in this  process so now with my iPad almost all  of these inefficiencies have been  resolved  I use procreate and I have a set of  templates that I use whenever I start a  project I start with my scale paper  template which I've set up in different  metric or imperial scales in different  paper sizes I have it on about 30  opacity and you can turn it off before  you print or you can leave it on if you  want we typically print in tabloid so  this is the one that I use the most and  because of these one meter grids I can  get a much higher level of accuracy much  faster even when I'm just doing a  schematic layout while I'm working on  projects with existing conditions I  would model the building and Revit based  on the existing drawings that are  available and use the PDF of the  drawings as the base the best thing  about using the iPad is the ability to  make options with layers and easily go  back if you make a mistake without  having to restart or erase things once  I've got the basic layout out I have a  set of scale furniture elements that I  can easily copy and paste into the  drawings or I use these stamp brush  tools which I have saved onto my  procreate app  when I'm developing the elevations I add  color and tone with a brush set that  kind of mimics markers and you can  overlay them and the lines get darker  and thicker and I feel like this just  adds a richness to the drawings  I've also been collecting different  background images that I've been using  to stylize the drawings and I don't use  this too much when I'm working but I  find that it just adds another level of  depth when presenting it digitally and  you can also play around with materials  with this as well when we move into 3D  as soon as the Design's been roughly  modeled in Revit I take a couple of  views of areas that needs to be resolved  and then I print a PDF of those views  and I use them as a base for drawing  over  and it's the same idea as the plans I  don't want to spend too much time  detailing one option before discussing  it with your team  you want to do a couple of iterations to  talk about the general direction of the  building  rather than spending all your time in  one option the problem with when you're  modeling things on the computer  especially in 3D especially in Revit is  that you tend to waste a lot of time  trying to resolve details that are  actually not that necessary and like  trying to make the image look nice and I  think there's something to be said about  the look of a 3D rendering like a rough  3D rendering in my opinion looks a lot  less presentable than a rough sketch  like a rough hand sketch even for  internal reviews a rough hand sketch by  its nature feels more like a work in  progress and it's more open for  discussion because with procreate you  can just simply go back if you make a  mistake your sketches end up coming out  a lot more refined and presentable than  a sketch over Trace  [Music]  another way I use my iPad is for site  reviews before I have my iPad I would  carry around like a full set of drawings  in one hand and my phone in the other  and I would be taking photos while at  the same time trying to make notes on my  drawings and a lot of the times I would  be flipping through the pages and  walking around and if you're a very  experienced architect who knows the ins  and outs of construction you might not  need the drawings as much but as a  fairly new architect you know I want to  double check everything and document  everything as someone who's always been  a little bit self-conscious about going  to slate  wall because I don't always look like I  belong there  I'm always kind of looking for ways for  me to look more confident and feel more  confident  and so the iPad has been great for that  because I can have all my information  right here  and during construction you will  inevitably come across things that you  don't really understand but that's  totally okay and that's what your team's  there for  but if you are on site alone or you're  still a fairly new architect one of the  best ways to build confidence is just to  be overly prepared like read all of the  rfis before you get to the meeting and  if you know that something's being built  while you're there try to anticipate the  problems before you get there and if I  don't understand something on site I'll  say something like can you explain the  problem from start to finish and you  might feel kind of stupid at the moment  but most people are more than happy to  walk you through the problem and it  actually makes you look more thorough  and like you're taking ownership of the  problem  I have my drawings and I upload them to  notability which is basically just like  a note-taking software and as I'm  walking around the site I take photos  and I insert them directly into the  pages and from there I can take  dimensions and write notes on the spot  [Music]  I'll also pull up click up on my split  screen which is where I organize all my  project notes and throughout the weeks I  will make notes on any concerns or  things I want to check out when I'm on  site and when I get there I can go  through the list and just check them off  sketching directly on the photos is also  the best way for me to communicate with  contractors or Consultants because they  don't really need pretty images they  just need the essential information but  usually they needed that information  like yesterday so doing a quick sketch  on my iPad is usually the fastest way  for me to send off that information  the most common way to use the iPad is  for markups we used to print tons and  tons of sheets of paper to mark up  construction sets but now we can just  mark it up on the iPad I use a simple  PDF program to annotate and I mean yeah  that's pretty much it if you're  interested in the design process and how  I develop concepts for a real project  I'll leave the video right here if  you're interested in any of these  graphic assets that I use with my iPad  setup I have fully downloadable  templates from scale grids to trees to  Furniture elements to people elements  and I'll leave the links to all of this  in the description there are also other  ways that I use my iPad for architecture  and also for non-architecture stuff so  if you have any specific questions or  want me to make a video on anything just  let me know down in the comments  remember to like this video if you found  it helpful and hit the Bell if you want  to be notified as soon as I release a  new video and if you haven't subscribed  subscribe and with that I'll see you  guys in the next video  foreign  [Music]
 

